.TH USERLS 1 "2026-10-23" "userls 2.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
userls \- read-only view of local accounts, groups and active sessions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B userls
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B userls info
\fINAME\fR|\fIUID\fR
.br
.B userls groups
[\fINAME\fR|\fIUID\fR]
.br
.B userls perms
[\fINAME\fR|\fIUID\fR]
.br
.B userls sessions
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B userls
lists the accounts known to the system through NSS
.RB ( pwd / grp ,
with a
.B getent
fallback), classifies them with a heuristic, and reports groups and active
sessions. It is strictly read-only: it never creates, modifies or deletes
accounts, passwords, groups or system files, and it never writes any file. It
does not need root privileges and never uses the network.
.PP
Without a subcommand,
.B userls
lists accounts. UID ranges are read from
.IR /etc/login.defs ;
sessions are read from
.BR who (1).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-type \fITYPE\fR
Only list accounts of the given type:
.BR normal ,
.BR service ,
.B system ,
.B unknown
or
.B all
(the default). Applies to the default listing and
.BR perms .
.TP
.B \-\-search \fITEXT\fR
Only list accounts whose name contains
.I TEXT
(case-insensitive), or whose UID equals
.I TEXT
when it is numeric.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Render the current result as JSON on stdout. Diagnostics stay on stderr, so
stdout parses as JSON.
.TP
.B \-\-no-color
Disable ANSI color. Color is only used on a terminal and is disabled when
the
.B NO_COLOR
environment variable is set.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the upstream version and exit.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print usage information and exit.
.PP
Shared output flags are accepted both before and after the subcommand, so
.B "userls \-\-json groups"
and
.B "userls groups \-\-json"
are equivalent.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B info \fINAME\fR|\fIUID\fR
Show the full record of one account: name, UID, GID, primary group, GECOS,
home, shell, heuristic type and the configured system UID range. Exits 1 when
no account matches.
.TP
.B groups \fR[\fINAME\fR|\fIUID\fR]
Without an argument, list every group with its GID and member list (empty
membership is shown as a dash). With an argument, list the groups of that
account, including the primary group even when the account is not listed as a
member, deduplicated and ordered by GID.
.TP
.B perms \fR[\fINAME\fR|\fIUID\fR]
Without an argument, list every account with its heuristic type, privilege
level and the mapped groups that granted that level. Rows are ordered by level
severity and then UID. With an argument, show one account's level and every
mapped group it holds, and exit 1 when no account matches. The
.B \-\-type
and
.B \-\-search
filters apply to the listing, not to a single-account report.
.TP
.B sessions
List active login sessions as reported by
.BR who (1):
user, terminal, login time and origin. An empty result exits 0.
.SH ACCOUNT TYPES
Classification is a heuristic, not an authoritative statement. Rules are
evaluated in order and the first match wins:
.IP "1." 4
UID 0 is
.BR system .
.IP "2." 4
A shell of
.IR /usr/sbin/nologin ,
.IR /sbin/nologin ,
.I /bin/false
or
.IR /usr/bin/false ,
or a home of
.IR "" ,
.IR / ,
.I /nonexistent
or
.IR /dev/null ,
is
.BR service .
.IP "3." 4
.IB UID_MIN " <= UID <= " UID_MAX
from
.I /etc/login.defs
is
.BR normal .
.IP "4." 4
.IB 1 " <= UID < " UID_MIN
is
.BR system .
.IP "5." 4
Anything else is
.BR unknown .
.PP
Home directories are not inspected with
.BR stat (2),
so a deleted home is not detected; this avoids hangs on dead autofs or NFS
mounts. Session times are printed in the C locale.
.SH PRIVILEGE LEVELS
.B userls perms
derives a privilege level from group membership. It is a group-based hint, not
an audit of sudoers, file modes or ACLs. Levels are evaluated in order and the
first match wins:
.IP "1." 4
UID 0 is
.BR root .
.IP "2." 4
Membership in
.B sudo
or
.B wheel
is
.BR sudo .
.IP "3." 4
Membership in
.BR adm ,
.BR disk ,
.BR docker ,
.BR lxd ,
.BR libvirt ,
.B shadow
or
.B systemd-journal
is
.BR privileged .
.IP "4." 4
Anything else is
.BR standard .
.PP
The primary group counts as a membership. Only mapped groups are reported, so
the output shows which groups produced the level.
.SH OUTPUT FORMATS
The default output is an aligned table with a trailing count line. JSON output
is a single object with
.BR schema_version ,
.B count
and a command key
.RB ( accounts ,
.B groups ,
.B sessions
or
.BR permissions ).
.B schema_version
is 1 and changes only on breaking output changes.
.B info \-\-json
returns the account object plus the configured system UID range.
.B perms \fINAME\fR \-\-json
returns the account object plus a
.B permission
object with
.B level
and
.BR groups .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including searches that match nothing.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime failure: unknown account, a failed
.B who
or
.BR getent ,
or a malformed source.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: unknown option or subcommand.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/login.defs
Source of the UID ranges. Missing or malformed values fall back to the
documented defaults (1000/60000/100/999) with a warning on stderr.
.TP
.I /etc/passwd ", " /etc/group
Read only indirectly, through NSS.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B userls
List every account.
.TP
.B userls \-\-type service
List service accounts.
.TP
.B userls \-\-search username
Search by name; a numeric argument matches the UID exactly.
.TP
.B userls info 1000
Show the account with UID 1000.
.TP
.B userls groups username
List the groups of
.IR username .
.TP
.B userls perms
List every account with its privilege level.
.TP
.B userls perms username
Show the privilege level and mapped groups of
.IR username .
.TP
.B userls sessions
List active sessions.
.TP
.B userls \-\-json
Machine-readable listing.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Enumeration is bound to what NSS exposes: backends that do not enumerate, such
as SSSD with
.IR "enumerate = false" ,
are invisible to both
.B userls
and
.BR getent .
Account types are heuristic. Sessions come from
.B who
and may be empty in containers or SSH setups without utmp records. The raw
.B who
line is preserved in the output so no data is invented. Privilege levels are
derived from a closed set of groups and never from sudoers, file modes or
ACLs, so they cannot see indirect escalation.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lslogins (1),
.BR who (1),
.BR getent (1),
.BR login.defs (5),
.BR passwd (5),
.BR group (5)
